1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:05,170
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:07,800
We have an upper intermediate lesson for you today.

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:08,310
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:09,790
And my name is Catherine.

5
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:14,330
So today we're talking about something that everyone who's ever lived away from home has felt before.

6
00:00:14,730 --> 00:00:15,010
Right.

7
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:16,980
And that's the word we're going to preview today.

8
00:00:16,980 --> 00:00:17,980
The word is homesick.

9
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:18,950
All right.

10
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:20,910
So let's take a look at that in today's language preview.

11
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:23,800
Vocabulary preview.

12
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:24,730
All right.

13
00:00:24,730 --> 00:00:25,510
So homesick.

14
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:28,250
If you feel homesick or you are homesick.

15
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:32,660
Well, yeah, you could say I'm homesick right now.

16
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:34,280
Or are you feeling homesick?

17
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:38,760
So it's a word that means I miss my home.

18
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:40,020
I miss my family.

19
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:43,200
I'm far away or I haven't seen my family in a long time.

20
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:44,840
And for some reason you just feel sad.

21
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:45,600
Okay.

22
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:47,720
So it's a feeling.

23
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:48,860
You feel a little bit depressed.

24
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:50,660
You feel sad because you're homesick.

25
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:51,760
Right.

26
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:52,760
Okay.

27
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:54,040
So this is what's happening today in our dialogue.

28
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:55,880
So let's listen to it for the first time.

29
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:05,440
Tom, how are you?

30
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:07,200
Hey, we missed you at the party last night.

31
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:07,760
Are you okay?

32
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:09,260
I don't know.

33
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:11,440
I didn't really feel like going out.

34
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:13,800
I guess I'm feeling a little homesick.

35
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:14,600
Come on.

36
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:16,180
We've been through this already.

37
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:21,960
Look, I know the adjustment was hard when you first got here, but we agree that you're going to try and deal with it.

38
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,240
I was.

39
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:28,920
It's just that the holidays are coming up and I won't be able to go home because I can't afford the airfare.

40
00:01:29,470 --> 00:01:35,010
I'm just longing for some of the comforts of home, like my mom's cooking and being around my family.

41
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:38,290
Yeah, it can get pretty lonely over the holidays.

42
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:43,370
When I first got here, I'd get depressed and nostalgic for anything that reminded me of home.

43
00:01:44,050 --> 00:01:47,310
I almost let it get to me, but then I started going out, keeping myself busy.

44
00:01:47,310 --> 00:01:49,190
And before I knew it, I was used to it.

45
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:52,810
I see what you mean, but I'm still bummed out.

46
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:55,070
Okay, how does this sound?

47
00:01:55,570 --> 00:01:58,130
Let's get you suited up and hit the dance club tonight.

48
00:01:58,590 --> 00:02:03,290
I hear that an awesome DJ is playing and there will be a lot of pretty single girls there.

49
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:05,910
You know, I could really go for that.

50
00:02:06,530 --> 00:02:08,750
You don't mind being my wingman for tonight?

51
00:02:09,210 --> 00:02:09,910
Not at all.

52
00:02:10,410 --> 00:02:11,050
It'll be fun.

53
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:13,070
It will be like a boys' night out.

54
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:15,400
Well, kind of.

55
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:16,400
Great.

56
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:17,030
I must warn you, though.

57
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:20,250
Whatever happens, don't let me go on a drinking binge.

58
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:22,570
Trust me, it's not a pretty picture.

59
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:30,060
All righty, we're back.

60
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,500
So yeah, I guess he's very homesick.

61
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:32,940
Very homesick.

62
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:34,420
And his friend is really worried.

63
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:36,040
Yeah.

64
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:37,440
But actually, I think this is common, right?

65
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:40,720
To be homesick during the holidays, like Christmas, for example.

66
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:48,840
Oh, man, it's the worst during the holidays because you think of all the traditions and all the things that you normally do at home and you're not doing them.

67
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:52,520
So this is something we're going to talk more about a little bit later on.

68
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:56,300
But let's take a look at some of the language that was used in Language Takeaway.

69
00:02:58,170 --> 00:02:58,990
Language Takeaway.

70
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:02,370
This first one is key, right?

71
00:03:02,650 --> 00:03:07,890
So when you move away from home or when you move to a new place, you have a period of adjustment.

72
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:15,340
So adjustment is kind of finding the right balance or doing something so that you can kind of feel comfortable.

73
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:19,740
OK, so you have to adjust to your new life, life away from home.

74
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:20,310
Or your new environment.

75
00:03:20,610 --> 00:03:24,880
So many times people say, wow, moving to China must be a big adjustment.

76
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:30,560
So it means there's so many differences that it must take a long time to feel comfortable again.

77
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:31,560
OK.

78
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:32,560
All right.

79
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:33,560
So that's adjustment.

80
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:34,560
What else do we have?

81
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:38,380
Well, we've got this next phrase that's really important, I think, but often gets overlooked.

82
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,480
We say the comforts of home, right?

83
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:42,340
Comforts of home.

84
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:44,140
So comfort's pretty easy, right?

85
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:45,680
Feeling comfortable.

86
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:46,840
So you have comfort.

87
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:50,600
But the comforts of home are things that are different with every person.

88
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:56,680
But those are things that you have at your home or your childhood home or your parents' home that make you feel good and feel happy.

89
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:59,820
So they're not only physical things or objects, right?

90
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:04,120
They can be like your mom's cooking or maybe even just spending time with your family.

91
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:09,040
Right, or reading the newspaper with your dad or things that you're used to that you don't have anymore.

92
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:11,040
And so those are the comforts of home.

93
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:14,040
I could say, Marco, do you miss the comforts of home in Ecuador?

94
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:17,080
I mean, do you have anything that's really...

95
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:17,720
Of course, of course.

96
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:25,520
I think one of the main things is actually not living in the city because I used to live in the valley a little bit further away and kind of almost countryside.

97
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:28,280
So that's one of the comforts of home that I miss.

98
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:31,080
Oh, you're making me nostalgic and I've never even been.

99
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:31,720
All right.

100
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:33,040
Well, this is our next word, actually.

101
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:34,720
Right, nostalgic.

102
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:35,020
Nostalgic.

103
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:35,540
It's a feeling.

104
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:38,340
I'm feeling nostalgic or he's just really nostalgic.

105
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:47,140
And so to be nostalgic is to kind of think about a time before or think positively about something that you experienced before.

106
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:48,910
So it's not usually a bad thing.

107
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:49,950
It's never a bad thing.

108
00:04:50,150 --> 00:04:53,890
Nostalgia is a good, warm, fond memory of something.

109
00:04:54,070 --> 00:04:55,150
Of something from your past.

110
00:04:55,490 --> 00:05:04,590
So you start thinking of something from your past that maybe brings nice, warm feelings, maybe a little bit sad because you miss those times, but it's usually thinking about the past.

111
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:06,400
Right.

112
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:07,400
So I have nostalgia for my childhood.

113
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:17,200
I loved, you know, summers in Chicago when it was hot and the kids would play in the sprinkler and I had my dog and you know, it's a happy feeling.

114
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:17,360
Right.

115
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:20,360
So nostalgia would be the noun and nostalgic would be the adjective.

116
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:21,840
Exactly.

117
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:22,840
All right.

118
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:26,550
And our last word for today on Language Takeaway is bummed out.

119
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:27,850
Bummed out.

120
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:28,830
I'm bummed out.

121
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:30,150
We've got another feeling here.

122
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:31,710
So we're all full of feelings today.

123
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:33,810
Bummed out is a sad feeling.

124
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:38,270
So to be bummed is kind of slang and it means to be sad.

125
00:05:38,730 --> 00:05:39,070
Okay.

126
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:39,770
Or disappointed.

127
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:41,840
Disappointed.

128
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:44,360
So you come into the office, Marco, and you look a little tired.

129
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:45,360
I said, are you okay?

130
00:05:45,390 --> 00:05:46,450
Are you bummed out about something?

131
00:05:47,130 --> 00:05:50,900
I can say, yeah, I lost my wallet last night so I'm really bummed out.

132
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:53,200
Or something like that.

133
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:56,140
So yeah, you feel bummed out when something bad or sad happens.

134
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:57,720
Right.

135
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:58,720
All right.

136
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:02,740
So let's move on now to a couple of different phrases on Fluency Builder.

137
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:05,540
Fluency Builder.

138
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:06,830
All right.

139
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:10,290
This first one is something that you often say to old friends or family.

140
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:14,510
You know, when you're friends with someone, they go through periods of happiness and sadness.

141
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:19,510
And so when someone's going through a down period again, you say, oh, we've been through this already.

142
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:21,050
We've been through this already.

143
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:26,590
Or you know, when you're giving someone advice and they never take your advice, you say, listen, we've been through this already.

144
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:30,120
You should know by now that I and then, you know, whatever the advice is.

145
00:06:30,130 --> 00:06:35,950
So this has happened before or we have experienced this before or we already solved this problem or situation.

146
00:06:36,650 --> 00:06:36,790
Right.

147
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:44,570
You could imagine two bank robbers who are going over their plan for the 10th time and then one says to the other, hey, we've been through this already.

148
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:46,530
You should know the plan by now.

149
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:47,030
Exactly.

150
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:48,840
All right.

151
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:49,470
So we've been through this already.

152
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:52,250
They were talking about the adjustments and everything.

153
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:54,610
And then what other phrase did they use?

154
00:06:55,030 --> 00:07:03,050
Well, the next couple of phrases have to do with the friend's suggestions because she has a lot of ideas to cheer her friend up, the depressed one.

155
00:07:03,430 --> 00:07:06,600
And the first thing she says is, hey, let's hit the dance club.

156
00:07:07,020 --> 00:07:10,020
Okay, let's hit the dance club or let's hit the dance floor tonight.

157
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:11,280
Right.

158
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:13,040
So hit in this case doesn't mean like hit.

159
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:15,320
It doesn't mean you're punching someone.

160
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:16,980
It means to go to or to do.

161
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:17,700
All right.

162
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:19,920
So we can say, let's go to the dance club tonight.

163
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:21,180
Let's hit the dance club.

164
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:21,680
Exactly.

165
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:22,780
It doesn't have to be a dance club.

166
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:24,360
But let's hit that concert tonight.

167
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:25,860
I really want to go there.

168
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:27,160
Okay.

169
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:28,520
So you can hit pretty much any place, right?

170
00:07:29,320 --> 00:07:30,320
Exactly.

171
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:32,640
And this is a very kind of spoken language.

172
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:37,160
So you wouldn't say this in your essays, but it's a good way to talk about plans with friends.

173
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:38,800
All right.

174
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:43,780
And our next word actually is kind of interesting because it's very colloquial and it's a wingman.

175
00:07:44,620 --> 00:07:45,840
I can be your wingman tonight.

176
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:50,460
So wingman, you have to think about wing meaning side, not like a bird's wing.

177
00:07:50,700 --> 00:08:03,520
So a wing means side and a wingman is a guy who will go with you to a bar or a club to kind of help you find a date or a girl or someone to talk to.

178
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:04,520
Okay.

179
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:15,280
So this person, this wingman usually keeps you company and helps you decide maybe what girl to talk to or maybe even introduces you to someone at a club or at a restaurant or a bar or whatever.

180
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:18,620
And, you know, the key is that people don't like to go alone to bars, right?

181
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:21,720
Because you feel sad and then you look weird because you're all alone.

182
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:23,560
And the same is true for girls.

183
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:30,080
And so if a guy wants to go to a bar and he sees two girls, well, he has to have a friend to talk to the other girl.

184
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:31,800
So wingman also helps in that sense.

185
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:33,380
He helps balance things.

186
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:34,000
All right.

187
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:36,360
So there's a whole science behind the wingman.

188
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:39,980
I'm sure if you look it up on Wikipedia, you'll find 10 pages of information.

189
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:40,920
All right.

190
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:45,960
So since they are going out tonight, they said it could be boys night out.

191
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:46,780
Okay.

192
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:52,620
And this is another phrase you hear all the time, especially among young people, boys night out and girls night out.

193
00:08:52,620 --> 00:08:59,080
And so, you know, sometimes you spend a lot of time with your husband or your wife or your boyfriend or girlfriend and you just want to have some time with your friends.

194
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:01,680
And so you call this girls night for me, girls night out.

195
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:02,960
You just go out and you do girl things.

196
00:09:03,420 --> 00:09:04,680
Guys night out, you do other things.

197
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:08,300
And so in this case, it's girls only or guys only.

198
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:09,600
Boys night out is only guys.

199
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:09,970
Exactly.

200
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:14,680
And that's why this is funny because it's the girl who says, hey, let's have boys night out.

201
00:09:14,680 --> 00:09:15,680
And she's not really a boy.

202
00:09:15,890 --> 00:09:16,650
All right.

203
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:17,010
Great.

204
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:23,770
And well, he agreed to go out and everything, but he warned her about him going on a drinking binge.

205
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:25,520
Okay.

206
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:26,520
Now, this is important.

207
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:29,400
A lot of people who are depressed or sad when they drink, they drink a lot.

208
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:33,370
And so binge means to do something in excess, to do it too much.

209
00:09:33,770 --> 00:09:34,050
Okay.

210
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:38,440
So a drinking binge would be drinking alcohol in excess, too much.

211
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:38,570
Too much.

212
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:39,750
So getting drunk right away.

213
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:43,520
And it's not healthy and we do not condone it.

214
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:44,520
Right.

215
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:45,520
Exactly.

216
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:46,520
Very good.

217
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:48,560
So we're going to stop with the phrases in this dialogue today.

218
00:09:48,620 --> 00:09:51,510
I think we should listen to it one more time and we'll be right back.

219
00:09:53,520 --> 00:10:00,760
Tom, how are you?

220
00:10:01,630 --> 00:10:03,130
Hey, we missed you at the party last night.

221
00:10:03,250 --> 00:10:03,690
Are you okay?

222
00:10:04,370 --> 00:10:05,210
I don't know.

223
00:10:05,830 --> 00:10:07,370
I didn't really feel like going out.

224
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:09,740
I guess I'm feeling a little homesick.

225
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:10,540
Come on.

226
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,160
We've been through this already.

227
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:15,320
Look, I know the adjustment was hard when you first got here.

228
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:17,900
But we agree that you're going to try and deal with it.

229
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:19,200
I was.

230
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:24,860
It's just that the holidays are coming up and I won't be able to go home because I can't afford the airfare.

231
00:10:25,340 --> 00:10:30,920
I'm just longing for some of the comforts of home, like my mom's cooking and being around my family.

232
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:34,270
Yeah, it can get pretty lonely over the holidays.

233
00:10:34,890 --> 00:10:39,330
When I first got here, I'd get depressed and nostalgic for anything that reminded me of home.

234
00:10:40,050 --> 00:10:43,270
I almost let it get to me, but then I started going out, keeping myself busy.

235
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:45,150
And before I knew it, I was used to it.

236
00:10:45,630 --> 00:10:46,850
I see what you mean.

237
00:10:47,270 --> 00:10:48,770
But I'm still bummed out.

238
00:10:49,410 --> 00:10:51,010
Okay, how does this sound?

239
00:10:51,530 --> 00:10:54,030
Let's get you suited up and hit the dance club tonight.

240
00:10:54,670 --> 00:10:59,230
I hear that an awesome DJ is playing and there will be a lot of pretty single girls there.

241
00:10:59,710 --> 00:11:01,870
You know, I could really go for that.

242
00:11:02,470 --> 00:11:04,770
You don't mind being my wingman for tonight?

243
00:11:05,230 --> 00:11:05,850
Not at all.

244
00:11:06,370 --> 00:11:07,010
It'll be fun.

245
00:11:07,630 --> 00:11:09,030
It will be like a boys' night out.

246
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:11,320
Well, kind of.

247
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:12,320
Great.

248
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:16,210
I must warn you, though, whatever happens, don't let me go on a drinking binge.

249
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:18,530
Trust me, it's not a pretty picture.

250
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:27,740
All right, we're back.

251
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,060
So speaking of homesick and holidays, do you actually get very depressed or homesick during Christmas?

252
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:34,320
I do.

253
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:41,320
Well, my family has a lot of Christmas traditions that are just really kind of wonderful and we do them every single year.

254
00:11:41,960 --> 00:11:47,000
And so the first time I spent Christmas away, I was living in Italy and it was kind of similar.

255
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,800
But then the second time was when I was living in China.

256
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:51,100
And China was just not the same.

257
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:52,940
No oven, so no Christmas cookies.

258
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:58,000
And no granny who, you know, pours vodka and eats meatballs.

259
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,950
And like my mom not running around the kitchen.

260
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:03,360
You feel lonely and then all of a sudden you get a phone call.

261
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:11,790
And it's the entire family and they're all together eating, having an amazing Christmas and they think it's going to help you feel less homesick, but it makes you feel more homesick.

262
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,440
So you definitely have nostalgia for those days during the holidays.

263
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:18,920
But what about you?

264
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:25,990
I rarely get homesick, but yeah, sometimes during Christmas or New Year's, New Year's we usually have more unique traditions in Ecuador.

265
00:12:26,800 --> 00:12:27,770
So it's very fun.

266
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,730
The family gets together, we dance, we eat.

267
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:36,200
So but yeah, I've been away from home so long that I rarely get homesick anymore.

268
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:38,150
But it's great to go back and spend time with the family.

269
00:12:38,430 --> 00:12:43,910
So after my visit to Ecuador, then I might get homesick for a little while, but then it goes away.

270
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:45,560
Yeah.

271
00:12:45,560 --> 00:12:46,600
Well, you guys should tell us.

272
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:47,720
Do you ever feel homesick?

273
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:50,000
And if you do, what do you feel homesick about?

274
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:50,440
What do you miss?

275
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:54,480
Maybe mom's cooking, maybe family, maybe the comforts of home.

276
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:56,880
Yeah, so we're curious to hear your answers.

277
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:58,840
We have a website, EnglishPod.com.

278
00:12:58,840 --> 00:13:00,300
You can of course also leave us questions.

279
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:02,180
We're happy to answer those.

280
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:03,520
And until next time, everyone.

281
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:04,200
Bye bye.
